ID: B3-Ch10a-Towers
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8) {
"What about the seredium towers?" Kate asks, stepping forward in the ether. "Now that the city is yours, what happens to the mages inside?"

"The Overseer protocol notes that no consensus was established regarding the towers prior to the engagement," the Overseer replies.

"I know," Kate says, her voice tightening. "That's why I'm asking now."

"Rest assured," the drone states. "The matters surrounding the towers will be resolved."

"What does that mean?" Kate demands. "Are you freeing the prisoners or keeping them locked in the machinery?"

"Query imprecise," the Overseer says. "The protocol cannot evaluate ambiguous prompts. Please rephrase."

"Stop playing games!" Kate snaps, her ethereal form radiating cold fury. "You know exactly what I'm asking. Will you free them or not?"

"Hostile tone detected," the Overseer says flatly. "Persisting in this posture will trigger defensive countermeasures. Current simulations project that Barry, Illuna, and Flower cannot shield your group from concentrated disintegration beams. You are vulnerable on this plane. Should you force a confrontation, the destruction of your companions will be your doing. Consider whether mages you have never met are worth the lives of those beside you."

Kate stands rigid, her jaw clenched, pale eyes burning with frustration.

"Time allocated for decision has elapsed," the Overseer drones. "Initiating disintegration sequence in three, two, one—"

"Wait!" Kate yells as the red-eyed drones charge their beams with blinding white heat. "Enough! I understand. I won't ask again. I'll... take your word that the towers will be addressed."

"The Overseer protocol appreciates your compliance," the machine says as the beams power down. "All assignments are executed with optimal efficiency. You will have no cause for grievance."

"Right," Kate whispers, looking drained. "I'm sure."

"Are there further inquiries, or does this conclude our business?"

"We are finished," Daren says heavily.

"Very well. Returning consciousness to material vessels."

A pulse of light snaps across my vision, and we are back in our flesh on the palace floor. The transition is seamless—no disorienting crawl back into our skulls.

"A pleasure conducting business," the Overseer's voice echoes from the green drone on the dais. "Should additional questions arise, contact any green patrol unit in the city. Responses will be delivered in due course."

"Right," Hadrik grunts. "Just like you answered us on the road here."

"Let's go," Daren says, turning toward the archway.

"If satisfied with municipal services, please record a positive evaluation with any green unit," the Overseer calls after us. "Your feedback improves city operations."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8) {
"What about the seredium towers?" Kate asks, stepping forward in the ether. "Now that the city is yours, what happens to the mages inside?"

"Operations proceed according to protocol," the Overseer replies. "Immediate measures will mitigate the pain of the captives. Concurrently, engineering teams will modify the intake matrices to support voluntary magical transfers. Once donors are secured through monthly stipends, the prisoners will be released in phased cohorts until the containment cells are empty."

"What is the estimated timeframe?" Kate asks.

"Under optimal conditions, two weeks," the Overseer says. "Accounting for variable complications, full decommission will require three to four weeks."

"Understood," Kate says quietly.

"Are there further inquiries, or does this conclude our business?"

"We are finished," Daren says heavily.

"Very well. Returning consciousness to material vessels."

A pulse of light snaps across my vision, and we are back in our flesh on the palace floor. The transition is seamless—no disorienting crawl back into our skulls.

"A pleasure conducting business," the Overseer's voice echoes from the green drone on the dais. "Should additional questions arise, contact any green patrol unit in the city. Responses will be delivered in due course."

"Right," Hadrik grunts. "Just like you answered us on the road here."

"Let's go," Daren says, turning toward the archway.

"If satisfied with municipal services, please record a positive evaluation with any green unit," the Overseer calls after us. "Your feedback improves city operations."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Feedback, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Feedback)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Feedback
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8) {
"So what now?" Hadrik grumbles as the heavy palace doors slam shut behind us. "We just walk away?"

"There's nothing else we can do," Daren says, boots ringing on the stones. "We helped the machine seize the throne. It's too late to reverse course."

"We could hunt down his core," Hadrik suggests, fist smacking into his open palm. "Smash it to scrap and install someone who isn't a tyrant or a mechanical puppet."

"The rebels never discovered the core's location," Daren says. "Even if we found it, the defenses would be impenetrable. A single sentry could cast us into the ether and disintegrate us before we drew a weapon. We have to be realistic. Our involvement in Ollendor's politics is finished."

"It sticks in my craw," Hadrik mutters. "Handing the kingdom to that smug tin can. But I agreed to the plan, so I carry my share of the blame."

"A cold ruler isn't necessarily a cruel one," Daren says. "As a construct, he won't fall prey to human greed or madness. Give it a few weeks before passing final judgment."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8) {
"So what now?" Hadrik grumbles as the heavy palace doors slam shut behind us. "We just walk away?"

"We wait," Daren says, boots ringing on the stones. "Our part in this is done. We chose our side; now we see if the machine honors his word."

"Doesn't it bother you?" Hadrik asks. "The way he spoke down to us in there?"

"I didn't back him for his manners," Daren answers calmly. "I backed him because his administration offered the best outcome for the people, and I believe that holds true. He spoke with arrogance, but he hasn't violated our terms. He deserves the benefit of the doubt."

"Fair enough," Hadrik concedes. "He promised to empty the towers. We'll see what the city looks like in a month."

}
As we step into the open courtyard, Flower gasps, floating upright within Melindra's air currents.

"I think I found it!" she shouts. "Melindra, put me down!"

"Sure," Melindra says, waving a hand to settle her gently on the paving stones.

"Thanks! Watch this!"

Before anyone can reply, Flower plants her bare feet and clenches her fists, her small arms trembling with effort. She draws in a deep breath, focusing inward. The fiery red glow in her irises recedes, replaced by her natural bright green, while the heat rippling off her skin cools to normal air.

She lets out a joyful laugh.

"I did it! I can control it! Look!"

She squeezes her fists again to trigger the transformation, and crimson sparks flare in her eyes. Mid-shift, the irises flash sapphire blue. Her right hand snaps up and delivers a sharp smack to her own forehead, snuffing the flames instantly.

"One minute!" Illuna's voice snaps from her mouth. "Can you not manage one single minute of restraint? What if you locked yourself in that state permanently? What then?"

"Sorry!" Flower chirps sheepishly. "I was just excited. I promise not to do it again!"

"You may practice after a full night's rest," Illuna says through the shared jaw. "You traversed the ether all morning. Pushing your limits now is foolish."

"Yes, Petal," Flower mumbles. "Understood."

"Good."

"Can I ask one tiny thing, though?" Flower asks.

"What?" Illuna says, full of suspicion.

"Now that the coronation is over," Flower says, bouncing on her toes, "can we visit that abandoned building Melindra mentioned? The one with the puzzle piece?"

"Unbelievable," Illuna sighs. "After everything today, you want to hunt puzzle pieces..."

choice(""Hey, that's not a bad idea. I've actually been meaning to ask about this too!"", B3-Ch10a-Puzzle, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Puzzle, v_b3_ch10_puzzle = 1)
choice(""I really couldn't care any less about those puzzle pieces right now."", B3-Ch10a-Puzzle, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Puzzle, v_b3_ch10_puzzle = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Intro-King
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 0) {
Four candidates took the stage: the king of the underground, two rebel regulars whose names I hadn't heard before today, and Albert.

Each stepped onto the wooden dais to deliver a brief speech before the voting began. The two unknown rebels made little impression on the assembly, drawing scattered applause at best. Neither poses any threat to the underground king's hold on power.

Our group stands near the back of the cavern hall, where we can talk without drawing stares from the crowd.

"Does Albert have an honest shot at this?" Hadrik asks. "Most folk see him as the king's shadow by now, don't they? Why elect the right hand when you can have the master?"

"Albert led his own rebel faction before aligning with the king, and the fighters haven't forgotten," Daren says. "Plenty of them would welcome a chance to restore him to independent authority."

"Maybe," Hadrik grunts. "We'll see soon enough."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 1) {
Four candidates took the stage: the king of the underground, two rebel regulars whose names I hadn't heard before today, and Peter.

Each stepped onto the wooden dais to deliver a brief speech before the voting began. The two unknown rebels made little impression on the assembly, drawing scattered applause at best. Neither poses any threat to the underground king's hold on power.

Our group stands near the back of the cavern hall, where we can talk without drawing stares from the crowd.

"Why would the king bother cheating?" Hadrik asks. "You don't think Peter stands a real chance against him, do you?"

"Peter is well-liked among the rank and file," Daren says. "If anyone in these caverns can mount a challenge against the king, it's him."

"Maybe," Hadrik grunts. "We'll see soon enough."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 2) {
Four candidates took the stage: the king of the underground, two rebel regulars whose names I hadn't heard before today, and Breynan.

Each stepped onto the wooden dais to deliver a brief speech before the voting began. The two unknown rebels made little impression on the assembly, drawing scattered applause at best. Neither poses any threat to the underground king's hold on power.

Our group stands near the back of the cavern hall, where we can talk without drawing stares from the crowd.

"Why would the king bother cheating?" Melindra asks. "Breynan has no charm, and those two last-minute volunteers have even less. The king has this won already."

"Probably," Daren says. "Still, one can hope."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 3 || v_b3_ch8_king == 4) {
Three candidates took the stage: the king of the underground and two rebel regulars whose names I hadn't heard before today.

Each stepped onto the wooden dais to deliver a brief speech before the voting began. The two unknown rebels made little impression on the assembly, drawing scattered applause at best. Neither poses any threat to the underground king's hold on power.

Our group stands near the back of the cavern hall, where we can talk without drawing stares from the crowd.

"Why would the king bother cheating?" Melindra asks. "He's the only viable choice on the stage. Who is going to vote for two strangers who stepped up five minutes ago?"

"Anyone who despises the king enough to vote for a coat rack instead," Daren says.

"And those protest votes will split between the two of them," Melindra counters. "Leaving the king right at the top. He can't lose."

"Probably," Daren admits. "Still, one can hope."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 5) {
Four candidates took the stage: the king of the underground, two rebel regulars whose names I hadn't heard before today, and Kelrim.

Each stepped onto the wooden dais to deliver a brief speech before the voting began. The two unknown rebels made little impression on the assembly, drawing scattered applause at best. Neither poses any threat to the underground king's hold on power.

Our group stands near the back of the cavern hall, where we can talk without drawing stares from the crowd.

"Does Kelrim win if the vote is fair?" Hadrik asks Daren.

"Without question," Daren says. "You heard the roar from the crowd when he announced his candidacy. To be honest, I expected the king to attempt an assassination by now. The only reason he hasn't is supreme confidence in his ability to tamper with the ballots. That's why we're watching."

"A fair point," Hadrik says. "Eyes open, then."

}
The ballot collectors pass our section, taking our slips and moving down the rows. We track their hands closely. Though the throng blocks our line of sight at times, none of the runners find an opportunity to swap bins unnoticed. Once gathered, the baskets are emptied onto the dais, where counters begin sorting slips under the scrutiny of neutral observers.

"Can we trust the monitors?" Hadrik asks. "Who picked them?"

"I checked," Daren says. "They are prominent figures who hold deep grudges against the king. They demanded oversight yesterday and haven't left the platform since. Tampering with the count under their noses is virtually impossible."

"So we can stand down?" Hadrik asks.

"We can," Daren says.

"Good," Hadrik breathes. "My neck was stiffening up."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Podium, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Podium)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Podium
TEXT:

"Melindra, drift me closer to the stage," Flower asks, floating within the swirling air currents as her eyes glow crimson. "I want to see the count!"

"The press is too dense," Melindra says. "My currents aren't delicate enough to weave you through this mob without cracking heads."

"Ugh..." Flower groans. "This would be simple if I could walk. Petal, let Melindra set me down for five minutes? Please?"

"No," Illuna says, the irises flashing vivid blue as she takes control. "We agreed you remain off your feet until you extinguish the ethereal fire. Physical exertion in this state risks burning out the vessel. If you wish to walk, focus on reverting to normal instead of complaining."

"I am focusing!" Flower protests. "I've been trying the whole time! Don't I get a short break?"

"You get a break when your eyes turn green," Illuna says flatly. "Back to work."

"Fine..." Flower mutters. "I'm trying."

"Don't you think you're pushing her too hard?" Kate asks as the eyes shift back to blue.

"I am keeping her alive," Illuna says, her expression hardening. "Every minute she lingers in that state increases the strain. If she cannot revert at will, the ethereal fire will consume her from the inside out. This is not something we can postpone."

"I know," Kate says quietly. "Still... can you really not guide her through it?"

"I cannot," Illuna says. "Ethereal physiology is foreign to me, and Arraka has offered nothing useful. Flower unlocked this form herself; only she can find the switch to close it."

"Just a thought," Hadrik interjects, scratching his beard, "but wouldn't Arraka be a bit more cooperative if you let her out of that box?"

"A possibility," Illuna says, glancing down at the golden amulet around her neck. A muffled buzzing rages against the enchanted metal. "Let us test your theory."

She flips the latch open a sliver.

"—and when I'm done with your families, I will kill you in the slowest, most painful way you can imagine!" Arraka's screech bursts into the hall, layered with half a dozen echoes. "Your eyes will melt from your face while your heads feel like they are exploding a thousand times per—"

Click. Illuna snaps the locket shut, cutting off the shriek.

"It seems your hypothesis requires revision," Illuna says, face utterly blank. "A brisk spin on the chain usually encourages cooperation. Does anyone care to volunteer?"

choice("Accept to spin Arraka's amulet.", B3-Ch10a-King-Spin, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-King-Spin, v_b3_ch10_spin = 1)
choice("Don't spin Arraka's amulet.", B3-Ch10a-King-Spin, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-King-Spin, v_b3_ch10_spin = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10a-King-Spin
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_spin == 0) {
We exchange glances, but nobody steps forward.

"Really?" Illuna says. "No volunteers? Not even Hadrik?"

"Arraka kept my Time God trial to herself," Hadrik says, looking away with a slight shrug. "Spinning her right now wouldn't sit right with me."

"She's been genuinely useful lately," Daren says. "I know it's because we're pointed at the same god, but with the coaching and the help with the underground plague, it almost feels like she's becoming a real part of this team."

"Part of the team?" Illuna's voice turns icy, and even the muffled rattling inside the amulet ceases. "You cannot be serious."

She looks from Daren to each of us in turn.

"Tell me the rest of you don't share this delusion."

"To be fair, she hasn't done anything to deserve a punishment right now," Hadrik says. "We were in the ether when you shut her away because she was grating on your nerves. Unless there's something we missed—"

"This is not about the chain!" Illuna snaps. "This is about calling that creature an ally! Do any of you truly believe she could ever stand on your side?"

"Illuna..." Kate says, stepping forward. "Like it or not, our goals align until the God of Time is dealt with. We agreed to take her instruction."

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_spin == 1) {
"Hand her over," I say, reaching out.

Illuna unhooks the chain and drops the amulet into my palm. I pinch the link and snap my wrist, setting the heavy gold spinning. The rotation blurs into a hum, and Arraka's muffled screams rise into a dizzy, frantic wail inside the casing.

"Barry," Kate says, her voice tight. "That's enough."

I slow the spin to a halt, surprised. "Since when do you defend Arraka?"

"I'm not defending her," Kate says. "I'm pointing out that antagonizing our only teacher before a fight with a god is reckless."

"She's teaching us because she wants Selkram dead as badly as we do," I say. "A little nausea won't make her walk away from revenge."

"Barry is correct on that point," Illuna says. "She will complain, but she will not abandon the lessons. Her vanity will not permit a petty grievance to spoil her grand scheme."

"It's not just the lessons, though," Daren says. "Her demeanor has shifted. Fewer threats, actual advice—she even helped me deal with the plague from the underground base. She's practically earning her place on this team."

"Her place on the team?" Illuna's eyes flare brilliant blue, and the amulet goes dead silent. "You cannot be serious."

She stares at Daren, then scans the rest of us.

"Tell me none of you believe that."

"She hasn't crossed us today," Hadrik says mildly. "We were still in the ether when you locked her up for shouting. Spinning her now feels uncalled for—"

"This is not about the chain!" Illuna cuts him off. "This is about calling that monster an ally! Do you genuinely believe she has changed?"

"Illuna," Kate says calmly. "Our interests align until the God of Time is dead. We agreed to use her knowledge."

}
"Accepting instruction and trusting her are entirely different matters," Illuna says, her jaw clenched. "I permitted her to teach us because I assumed you understood what she is. She is not an ally. She is a weapon we are holding by the blade. Tell me you understand that."

"Nobody is suggesting we become lifelong companions," Daren says gently. "Only that she has shown a degree of progress."

"She has not changed!" Illuna's composure shatters, her voice echoing off the stone walls. "She will never change! She is the same ruthless, sadistic and manipulative mass murderer she has always been. You cannot afford to treat her as an ally. You cannot even consider that possibility. Do not repeat my mistake! You will regret it for as long as you live!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Speech, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Speech)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Speech
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_spin == 0) {
"Alright, alright, we hear you," Hadrik says, raising his hands to placate her. "We'll keep our guard up. No more talk about teammates. Right, everyone?"

We nod in silence. Illuna rarely loses her temper, and whenever the mask slips, Arraka is always at the center of it. Given what the banshee did to her centuries ago, the fury is hardly unearned.

"Good," Illuna says, her voice dropping back to its cold, measured cadence. "As long as we understand each other. Let us watch the dais. The announcement is coming."

She turns toward the stage, and we turn our attention back to the election in silence.

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_spin == 1) {
"Alright, alright, we hear you," Hadrik says, raising his hands to placate her. "We'll keep our guard up. No more talk about teammates. Right, everyone?"

We nod in silence. Illuna rarely loses her temper, and whenever the mask slips, Arraka is always at the center of it. Given what the banshee did to her centuries ago, the fury is hardly unearned.

"Good," Illuna says, her voice dropping back to its cold, measured cadence. "As long as we understand each other. Let us watch the dais. The announcement is coming."

She turns toward the stage, and we turn our attention back to the election in silence.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 0) {
Moments later, the head monitor steps to the edge of the platform and bellows the final tally. To our collective shock, Albert carries the vote.

"Albert won?" Hadrik blinks as the former rebel leader mounts the steps to deliver his address. "Him? Of all people?"

"I warned you not to dismiss his reputation," Daren says. "The underground remembers who led them before the king took over."

"Either that," Hadrik mutters, "or they truly despise the king."

Albert's victory speech is brief and workmanlike, greeted by a roar of applause and stomping boots. As the assembly disperses into the side tunnels, the king of the underground catches our eye. He gives a curt nod from across the floor, then weaves through the departing crowd toward our corner.

"I wondered if you would attend," the king says, stopping before us. "Did the proceedings meet your standards?"

"Better than expected," Daren replies. "None of us anticipated Albert taking the crown."

"You didn't?" The king raises a brow. "Albert has commanded respect here for years. I anticipated a tight contest."

"We assumed you'd pull a trick from your sleeve," Hadrik says bluntly. "Rig the tally rather than yield the throne."

"You believed I would falsify the vote?" Genuine offense sharpens the king's tone. "I knew your opinion of me was low, but this borders on slander."

"Don't play the martyr," I say. "You ordered our assassination."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 1) {
Moments later, the head monitor steps to the edge of the platform and bellows the final tally. To our surprise, Peter carries the vote.

"Peter took it?" Hadrik grins as Peter climbs the platform steps, looking somewhat overwhelmed by the cheers. "A pleasant turn of events! How did he manage that?"

"I told you he commanded goodwill among the fighters," Daren says. "He may seem hesitant in private council, but the rank and file trust him. Clearly there is more spine there than we saw."

"Anyone is an improvement over the king," Hadrik says, "and Peter is an honest lad. Good on him."

"Indeed," Daren agrees. "Of all our choices, he is the best outcome."

"Let's hope he maintains that independence," Kate says quietly, "and doesn't take secret orders from the king once crowned."

"Have faith," Hadrik says, clapping her shoulder. "He'll do well."

Peter's victory speech is brief and heartfelt, greeted by a roar of applause and stomping boots. As the assembly disperses into the side tunnels, the king of the underground catches our eye. He gives a curt nod from across the floor, then weaves through the departing crowd toward our corner.

"I wondered if you would attend," the king says, stopping before us. "Did the proceedings meet your standards?"

"Better than expected," Daren replies. "None of us anticipated Peter taking the crown."

"You didn't?" The king raises a brow. "Peter has commanded respect here for years. I anticipated a tight contest."

"We assumed you'd pull a trick from your sleeve," Hadrik says bluntly. "Rig the tally rather than yield the throne."

"You believed I would falsify the vote?" Genuine offense sharpens the king's tone. "I knew your opinion of me was low, but this borders on slander."

"Don't play the martyr," I say. "You ordered our assassination."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 2 || v_b3_ch8_king == 3 || v_b3_ch8_king == 4) {
Moments later, the head monitor steps to the edge of the platform and bellows the final tally. As expected, the king of the underground takes the election by a wide margin.

Daren watches the king take the podium, disappointment written plainly across his face.

"We anticipated the outcome," Daren murmurs, "yet it remains disheartening."

"Perhaps he will surprise us," Hadrik offers. "Arrogance does not preclude effective leadership."

"His competence isn't in question; his morality is," Daren says. "Still, the fault is ours for failing to field a compelling alternative. We cannot complain."

"We arrived scant days ago," Hadrik notes. "We barely know these people. Better we didn't meddle further."

"Perhaps," Daren sighs.

The king's speech is polished and commanding, met with dutiful applause and cheering from the crowd. As the assembly filters out, the king catches our eye, nods across the floor, and walks toward our section.

"I wondered if you would attend," the king says, coming to a halt before us. "Did the proceedings satisfy your curiosity?"

"Hardly as much as yours," Hadrik grunts.

"I understand I was not your preferred candidate," the king says smoothly, "but I assure you the city will prosper under my rule."

"Prosper for whom?" Hadrik counters.

"For Ollendor," the king replies.

"Right," Hadrik mutters. "Naturally."

"So now that you've secured the crown," I say, "can we stop watching our backs, or are your assassins still on retainer?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 5) {
Moments later, the head monitor steps to the edge of the platform and bellows the final tally. To everyone's astonishment, Kelrim wins by a decisive margin.

"Kelrim won!" Hadrik shouts over the roar of the crowd as the goblin ascends the dais. "The little fellow actually pulled it off!"

"I never dared hope it would be so clean," Daren says, staring in disbelief. "Did the king truly make no attempt to tamper with the ballots?"

"Perhaps he grew overconfident," Hadrik suggests.

"Impossible," Daren says. "He witnessed the support Kelrim commanded yesterday. He couldn't have been blind to the threat. Could we have misjudged him?"

"The day isn't over," Hadrik cautions. "Keep your eyes open."

Kelrim's acceptance speech is earnest and stumbling, met with thunderous cheers from the goblin quarter and widespread applause from the rebel ranks. As the assembly disperses, the king of the underground notices us, nods across the hall, and walks over.

"I wondered if you would attend," the king says, stopping before us. "Did the outcome meet your approval?"

"It did," Daren says, "though I confess my surprise. How did Kelrim secure the victory? I understood you opposed a goblin monarch."

"I am hardly thrilled by the result," the king says with a thin smile. "What did you expect me to do? Butcher him?"

"Well... yes," Daren says. "Isn't that your customary solution?"

"You believed I would have the goblin murdered?" Genuine shock hardens the king's expression. "Good gods. I knew your opinion of me was low, but this exceeds all expectation."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Morning, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Morning)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Morning
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_king != 5) {
"As I explained, that was a misunderstanding," the king says. "I never instructed Albert to take your lives."

"You told him to handle us 'discreetly,'" I counter.

"And wanton slaughter strikes you as discreet?" The king raises an eyebrow. "Every soul in this underground knows Albert executes my high-value contracts. Had you vanished under suspicious circumstances, every finger would point directly at me."

"Is that so?" Hadrik asks. "What were his orders, then?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 5) {
"So the thought of killing Kelrim never crossed your mind?" Hadrik asks.

"Of course not!" the king insists. "I have ordered executions, yes, but only against individuals who presented an active, catastrophic threat to Ollendor—and never within my own domain. If I butchered every rival who crossed my path, no rebel leader would have ever allied with me."

"Don't act like a saint," I say. "You planned to have us assassinated."

"As I explained, that was a misunderstanding," the king says. "I never instructed Albert to take your lives."

"You told him to handle us 'discreetly,'" I counter.

"And wanton slaughter strikes you as discreet?" The king raises an eyebrow. "Every soul in this underground knows Albert executes my high-value contracts. Had you vanished under suspicious circumstances, every finger would point directly at me."

"Is that so?" Hadrik asks. "What were his orders, then?"

"To dismantle your credibility," the king explains. "Albert was to circulate rumors of your collusion with the Overseer, eroding public trust in case you moved to sabotage the election. That plan was contingent on active interference, which you wisely avoided."

"What about the seredium towers?" Kate cuts in. "What happens to the mages trapped inside?"

"You must direct that question to our newly anointed goblin sovereign," the king says with a dry chuckle. "His policies remain entirely unmapped. We shall see what he devises."

"Right," Kate murmurs.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 0) {
"To dismantle your credibility," the king explains. "Albert was to circulate rumors of your collusion with the Overseer, eroding public trust in case you moved to sabotage the election. That plan was contingent on active interference, which you wisely avoided."

"What about the seredium towers?" Kate cuts in. "What happens to the mages trapped inside?"

"That rests on Albert's discretion," the king says. "Though I expect he will follow our agreed plan. We will free the kin of underground citizens first, then rescue the most vulnerable captives—children and the elderly—without draining the city's power completely. For the remainder, we will hasten our research into magical energy alternatives to bring a swift end to their confinement."

"I see," Kate says quietly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 1) {
"To dismantle your credibility," the king explains. "Albert was to circulate rumors of your collusion with the Overseer, eroding public trust in case you moved to sabotage the election. That plan was contingent on active interference, which you wisely avoided."

"What about the seredium towers?" Kate cuts in. "What happens to the mages trapped inside?"

"That rests on Peter's discretion," the king says. "Though I expect he will follow our agreed plan. We will free the kin of underground citizens first, then rescue the most vulnerable captives—children and the elderly—without draining the city's power completely. For the remainder, we will hasten our research into magical energy alternatives to bring a swift end to their confinement."

"I see," Kate says quietly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 2 || v_b3_ch8_king == 3 || v_b3_ch8_king == 4) {
"To dismantle your credibility," the king explains. "Albert was to circulate rumors of your collusion with the Overseer, eroding public trust in case you moved to sabotage the election. That plan was contingent on active interference, which you wisely avoided."

"What about the seredium towers?" Kate cuts in. "What happens to the mages trapped inside?"

"Everything proceeds according to the blueprint I outlined," the king states. "We will free the kin of underground citizens first, then rescue the most vulnerable captives—children and the elderly—without draining the city's power completely. For the remainder, we will hasten our research into magical energy alternatives to bring a swift end to their confinement."

"I see," Kate says quietly.

}
"Now, if you will excuse me," the king says, adjusting his cloak, "matters of state require my attention. You have full run of the caverns, though our administration will relocate to the surface posthaste. It will be pleasant to wake to sunlight after all these years below ground. Farewell."

"Until later," Daren says as the king turns and strides toward the council chambers.

"Well," Daren murmurs once the king is out of earshot. "Perhaps our initial assessment of the man was too severe."

choice(""You may be right."", B3-Ch10a-Prisoners, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Prisoners, v_b3_ch10_wrong = 0)
choice(""Nah, I think he was lying through his teeth about not planning to kill us."", B3-Ch10a-Prisoners, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Prisoners, v_b3_ch10_wrong = 1)
