#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 5) {
Kate steps forward, hesitation in her posture. "Kelrim... this may be sudden, but there is an urgent matter. Are you aware of the mages held in the seredium towers?"

"Yes," Kelrim says, sorrow clouding his features. "Breynan briefed me. Those poor souls... to endure such torment while the city above moves on..."

"What do you intend to do with them once installed in the palace?" Kate asks.

"Free every last one," Kelrim says firmly. "Immediately. Their suffering ends now."

"Have you discussed that with the underground king?" Kate asks. "His position was that releasing all prisoners at once would cut off the city's magical power, grounding every flying carriage and halting the city's machinery. He planned to free only rebel relatives and leave the rest until another power source was found."

"Unacceptable," Kelrim says, shaking his head with sudden resolve. "Freedom cannot be rationed. If darkness falls on the streets, we shall navigate it together. I will confront the king at once!"

"Allow me to mediate that discussion, your majesty," Sir Horace interjects gently. "I have known him for decades. He will resist initially, but he understands practical compromise. I will ensure he cooperates."

"Thank you, Sir Horace," Kelrim says. "I place the negotiation in your capable hands."

"Remember not to sever the chains by brute force!" Kate warns. "The backlash will kill them. The extraction mechanisms in the control room must be deactivated first."

"Understood," Sir Horace nods. "Now, your majesty, our first war council awaits. If you will accompany me."

"Lead on, Sir Horace," Kelrim says, offering us a quick bow. "Farewell, my friends. We shall speak again soon."

"You see?" Daren tells Kate as the goblin departs. "Your worries were unfounded. The prisoners are in good hands."

"Yes," Kate says, a faint, relieved breath escaping her lips. "I'm glad."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king != 5) {
"Didn't the king explain that all prisoners will eventually be freed?" Daren asks. "It is merely a matter of time and preparation."

"And if they never find an alternate energy source?" Kate counters. "Do those mages rot in the machinery forever?"

"We must trust they will honor their word," Daren says gently. "We chose to back the rebellion; we have to grant them the latitude to govern."

"You're right," Kate murmurs, looking down. "We made our choice. I only hope we don't live to regret it."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Surface, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Surface)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Surface
TEXT:

"So what's next?" Hadrik asks, stretching his thick arms. "Do we linger in the caverns, or head up?"

"Let's visit the surface market for supplies," Daren suggests. "It may be weeks before we reach another major trading hub."

"Agreed," Kate says. "The teleporter chamber should be active. Barry Thundercloud's replacement was heading that way."

We spend ten minutes walking through the stone corridors, hashing out the election results. In the transport room, the new technician keys the coordinates and sends us up in a flash of light, depositing our group in the center of the upper market district.

A handful of citizens have already ventured out following the lift of the lockdown, browsing merchant stalls beneath the afternoon sun. As we debate which apothecary to visit first, Flower gasps, floating upright within Melindra's air currents.

"I think I found it!" she shouts. "Melindra, put me down!"

"Sure," Melindra says, waving a hand to settle her gently on the paving stones.

"Thanks! Watch this!"

Before anyone can reply, Flower plants her bare feet and clenches her fists, her small arms trembling with effort. She draws in a deep breath, focusing inward. The fiery red glow in her irises recedes, replaced by her natural bright green, while the heat rippling off her skin cools to normal air.

She lets out a joyful laugh.

"I did it! I can control it! Look!"

She squeezes her fists again to trigger the transformation, and crimson sparks flare in her eyes. Mid-shift, the irises flash sapphire blue. Her right hand snaps up and delivers a sharp smack to her own forehead, snuffing the flames instantly.

"One minute!" Illuna's voice snaps from her mouth. "Can you not manage one single minute of restraint? What if you locked yourself in that state permanently? What then?"

"Sorry!" Flower chirps sheepishly. "I was just excited. I promise not to do it again!"

"You may practice after a full night's rest," Illuna says through the shared jaw. "You traversed the ether all morning. Pushing your limits now is foolish."

"Yes, Petal," Flower mumbles. "Understood."

"Good."

"Can I ask one tiny thing, though?" Flower asks.

"What?" Illuna says, full of suspicion.

"Now that the election is over," Flower says, bouncing on her toes, "can we visit that abandoned building Melindra mentioned? The one with the puzzle piece?"

"Unbelievable," Illuna sighs. "After everything today, you want to hunt puzzle pieces..."

choice(""Hey, that's not a bad idea. I've actually been meaning to ask about this too!"", B3-Ch10a-Puzzle, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Puzzle, v_b3_ch10_puzzle = 1)
choice(""I really couldn't care any less about those puzzle pieces right now."", B3-Ch10a-Puzzle, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Puzzle, v_b3_ch10_puzzle = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Puzzle
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_puzzle == 1) {
"Hey, that's not a bad idea. I've actually been meaning to ask about this too!"

"You want the fragments as well?" Hadrik asks. "What makes these trinkets so special?"

"No idea," I say. "Which makes finding out worthwhile."

"You're coming along?" Flower beams. "Wonderful! Melindra, where are we headed?"

"Hold on," Daren says, stepping between us. "You aren't flying off unescorted, are you?"

"Why not?" Flower asks. "The siege is broken. The streets are calm."

"The battle ended, but royalist stragglers remain in the city," Daren insists. "What if loyal guards seek vengeance for their dead king?"

"Loyrang probably fled the gates an hour ago," I say, "and that illusionist couldn't crack an egg without a distraction."

"Those officers trained apprentices," Daren says. "We should stay together."

"Walking across the district takes hours," Melindra says. "Air currents take minutes."

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_puzzle == 0) {
"I really couldn't care any less about those puzzle pieces right now."

"Then you won't get any, but I am going!" Flower announces. "Melindra, lead the way!"

"Hold on," Daren says, stepping between us. "You aren't flying off unescorted, are you?"

"Why not?" Flower asks. "The siege is broken. The streets are calm."

"The battle ended, but royalist stragglers remain in the city," Daren insists. "What if loyal guards seek vengeance for their dead king?"

"Loyrang probably fled the gates an hour ago," I say, "and that illusionist couldn't crack an egg without a distraction."

"Those officers trained apprentices," Daren says. "We should stay together."

"Walking across the district takes hours," Melindra says. "Air currents take minutes."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"Can you not ferry the entire party?" Daren asks.

"I can, but dragging that much dead weight slows us to a crawl," Melindra says. "We split up. Those of us who can fly make a quick run to the ruin; the rest of you handle the supply shopping. We meet back in this market in an hour or two. Agreed?"

"Those who can fly..." Daren considers the suggestion. "That leaves me, Hadrik, and Leila for provisions."

"Right," Melindra says. "Kate, your ice platforms handle distance flight as long as you travel light, correct?"

"Easily," Kate says.

"Then let's move," Melindra says, weaving hand motions to gather a cushion of circulating wind around me. "Follow my lead and watch the ground for ranged casters. Ready?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
"Can you not ferry the entire party?" Daren asks.

"I can, but dragging that much dead weight slows us to a crawl," Melindra says. "We split up. Those of us who can fly make a quick run to the ruin; the rest of you handle the supply shopping. There's a market square right around the corner. We meet you there in an hour or two. Agreed?"

"Those who can fly..." Daren considers the suggestion. "That leaves me, Hadrik, and Leila for provisions."

"Right," Melindra says. "Kate, your ice platforms handle distance flight as long as you travel light, correct?"

"Easily," Kate says.

"Then let's move," Melindra says, weaving hand motions to gather a cushion of circulating wind around me. "Follow my lead and watch the ground for ranged casters. Ready?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_puzzle == 1) {
"Ready," I say. Kate and Flower nod in turn.

"Up we go," Melindra calls.

We launch into the sky, our separate magics taking the air. We wave a brief goodbye to Daren, Hadrik, and Leila below and bank toward the northern district. With the wind roaring past our ears, conversation gives way to concentration as Melindra charts the course over slate rooftops and spires.

Halfway to the coordinates, a violent jolt of vertigo seizes my skull.

Vision tears. My perspective flips violently into the ethereal plane, plopping my bodiless soul into the hollow cube of my sanctuary. A breath later, the material plane snaps back into focus—and I realize I've lost hold of the air currents.

I'm in free fall.

Wind screams in my face as the ground rushes up. I throw my arms outward, forcing will into the spell. The air currents catch with a sharp jerk, arresting my descent fifty feet above jagged gables.

I hover there, pulse hammering against my ribs, cold sweat prickling my brow.

It's happening again. The plane-shift wasn't an isolated fluke.

"Barry?" Melindra circles back, scanning me. "Did the lift slip?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_puzzle == 0) {
"I'm ready," I say. "Though I'm still not taking a trinket."

"Good, because I wasn't going to share one anyway!" Flower retorts, sticking out her tongue.

"Up we go," Melindra calls.

We launch into the sky, our separate magics taking the air. We wave a brief goodbye to Daren, Hadrik, and Leila below and bank toward the northern district. With the wind roaring past our ears, conversation gives way to concentration as Melindra charts the course over slate rooftops and spires.

