It's happening again. The plane-shift wasn't an isolated fluke.

"Barry?" Melindra circles back, scanning me. "Did the lift slip?"

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Refreshingly, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Refreshingly)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Refreshingly
TEXT:

Everyone is staring at me. Kate hovers on a floating disc of ice, her brow furrowed.

Do I tell them?

No. It will only sow panic. My mind is likely struggling to settle after being ripped back and forth through the ether all morning. Give it a day, and the disorientation will pass.

"I'm fine," I say, steadying my breathing. "Testing a high-speed bank and caught the edge of an updraft. All clear."

"Don't get creative over the rooftops," Melindra warns. "The gables down there will impale you before you hit the street."

"Noted," I say, pulling back into formation.

We bank north again, skimming over chimneys and towers. The dizziness does not return.

A few rogue slips between planes won't kill me. I'll worry if the intervals tighten.

"Coming in," Melindra signals, banking toward a weathered two-story structure nestled between crooked alleys. Unlike Billy Bob's garish architectural experiments, this townhouse is plain stone and timber, blending quietly into the city fabric.

"Still standing," Melindra says as we touch down in the courtyard. "I worried they had cleared the lot. Follow me inside; the mechanism to the lower chambers is intact."

"Lower chambers?" Flower asks, trotting through the doorway. "I thought the puzzle piece was in the house!"

"I said you had to enter the building to reach it," Melindra corrects her, stepping over fallen plaster. "I never said it sat on the kitchen counter. It's an old ruin. I know the lever sequence by heart. Five minutes, and we're out."

"What if your memory is off?" Kate asks.

"My memory is fine," Melindra says, dropping to a crouch and depressing three flagstones in rapid succession. "I've run this sequence before."

Gears grind beneath the masonry. A square section of the floor retracts, revealing a narrow stone staircase descending into darkness.

"See?" Melindra smirks. "Down we go."

"Are there blue crystals below, or do we need fire?" Flower asks.

"No crystals," Melindra says. "Wall sconces should be intact."

"On it!" Flower conjures three hovering orbs of flame that drift ahead, casting golden light across damp stone steps.

At the bottom, Flower uses the fireballs to ignite the iron torches lining the walls, then dismisses her spells.

"That is a lot of levers..." Flower murmurs, eyes wide as she takes in the iron handles protruding from the corridor walls at regular intervals.

"Pay attention," Melindra says. "The sequence requires precise timing between pulls. I'll handle the toggles. When I reach the final switch, whoever wants a puzzle piece needs to be positioned at the far wall—the release hatch only opens for a few seconds. Understood?"

"Understood!" Flower says.

"Right," Melindra says. "Here goes."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Awkward, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Awkward)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Awkward
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1 && v_b3_ch8_heartfelt != 2 && v_ch11_romance_rose != 1 || v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1 && v_b3_ch8_heartfelt != 2 && v_ch11_saved_rose != 1) {
Melindra steps down the hall, pulling levers in a deliberate cadence. After setting three iron handles in place, she walks back to our group.

"Now we wait about a minute," Melindra says, leaning against the damp masonry.

"Understood," Flower says.

Silence settles over the corridor. Ten seconds pass without a sound save the crackle of torch pitch.

"So..." Melindra says, looking from me to Kate with an arched brow. "Are the two of you courting yet, or are we still pretending?"

"What?" Kate's face heats instantly. "Where did that come from? Why would you ask that?"

"Oh, please, Kate!" Melindra laughs. "It's written all over you. Why not admit it? You'd feel far lighter if you spoke the truth."

"I don't know what you're talking about," Kate says, her voice strained as she tries to hold her usual composure. "There is nothing between Barry and me."

"Is that right?" Melindra smirks. "So you despise him?"

"I never said I despise him," Kate mutters.

"So you like him?" Melindra presses.

"I didn't say that either!"

"Then you do like him," Melindra says, grinning.

"Can you stop pestering me and pull the next lever?" Kate snaps, looking at the ceiling. "Your minute is up."

"Alright, alright," Melindra chuckles, pushing off the wall. "No need to flare up. I'll hold my tongue... for now."

As Melindra heads down the passage, Kate stands rigid, her cheeks glowing crimson in the torchlight.

"What?" she glares at me when she catches my eye.

"Nothing," I say innocently. "Not a word."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_heartfelt == 2 && v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1 && v_ch11_romance_rose != 1 || v_b3_ch8_heartfelt == 2 && v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1 && v_ch11_saved_rose != 1) {
Melindra steps down the hall, pulling levers in a deliberate cadence. After setting three iron handles in place, she walks back to our group.

"Now we wait about a minute," Melindra says, leaning against the damp masonry.

"Understood," Flower says.

Silence settles over the corridor. Ten seconds pass without a sound save the crackle of torch pitch.

"So..." Melindra turns to me with a knowing smirk. "You and Leila, hm?"

"What about us?" I say, keeping my expression blank.

"Oh, don't play innocent," Melindra says. "The pair of you have been tiptoeing around each other since your last night shift. Something clearly transpired."

"Of course..." Kate murmurs, eyes widening with sudden realization. "Now it makes sense."

"What makes sense?" I ask.

"Before your watch, Leila asked me repeatedly whether anything romantic existed between you and me," Kate explains. "She made me confirm it three times. She wanted to be certain she wouldn't hurt me if the two of you grew close."

"That was thoughtful of her," I murmur.

"She cares deeply for you," Kate says softly. "Do not let her down."

"I won't," I promise.

"Back in a moment," Melindra says, stepping away. "Next lever."

She walks down the passage and pulls an iron handle on the left wall.

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1) {
Melindra steps down the hall, pulling levers in a deliberate cadence. After setting three iron handles in place, she walks back to our group.

"Now we wait about a minute," Melindra says, leaning against the damp masonry.

"Understood," Flower says.

Silence settles over the corridor. Ten seconds pass without a sound save the crackle of torch pitch.

"You know, Barry..." Melindra says, turning to me. "I've wondered for a while... does Leila believe you and I are a couple?"

"She does," I answer. "When I met her father, she introduced us as partners. We gave her that impression."

"Why would she assume that?" Melindra asks, genuinely puzzled. "Because we slept together?"

"That usually factors into people's conclusions, yes," I say dryly.

"Hm," Melindra considers this. "Look, I have nothing against you. It's simply been my rule for centuries not to form deep attachments. Mortal companions age, wither, and leave me behind. It's nothing personal."

"Understood," I say. "No offense taken."

Another stretch of silence follows.

"Though there was one instance, centuries back..." Melindra muses. "I traveled with a man for a few years. We were... somewhat together, without the weight. We shared a tent and our time, but kept no long-term claims. The only rule was exclusivity while the arrangement lasted. No further obligations."

"Isn't that just a relationship?" I ask.

"No!" Melindra says sharply. "There were no strings. Either of us could walk away tomorrow with no bitter words. We remained friends long after. It was a practical arrangement."

She pauses, looking straight into my eyes.

"Would you be open to something similar?"

"You and me?" I clarify. "Traveling together, sharing a tent, no strings attached?"

"Yes," Melindra says simply, awaiting my response.

}
#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 1 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 1 && v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1) {
Melindra steps down the hall, pulling levers in a deliberate cadence. After setting three iron handles in place, she walks back to our group.

"Now we wait about a minute," Melindra says, leaning against the damp masonry.

"Understood," Flower says.

Silence settles over the corridor. Ten seconds pass without a sound save the crackle of torch pitch.

"Barry," Melindra says, turning toward me. "What is the situation between you and that herbalist from Thilias? Are you two courting or not?"

"In a sense," I say.

"He promised to return to her after the tournament concludes," Kate supplies.

"Hadrik mentioned Rose told everyone it was only one night," Melindra notes. "Doesn't that imply her interest was brief?"

"You misunderstand," Kate says. "I spoke with Rose privately. She framed it as a single night because she refused to saddle Barry with guilt while he fought in the arena. She feared confessing her feelings would make him abandon his dream of becoming a mage."

"So she truly loves him?" Melindra asks.

"Without question," Kate says.

"Understood," Melindra nods. "Good to know."

"You're welcome," Kate says.

"Time for the next lever," Melindra says, walking down the passage to engage a handle on the left wall.

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1) {
A spike of agonizing pressure pierces my skull.

Vision violently shatters. My consciousness ricochets between the earthen and ethereal planes, flashing back and forth in rapid succession like a strobe light. Just as the rhythm slows, another surge hits—tossing my perspective between flesh and void more than ten times in a single heartbeat.

"Barry?" Kate grabs my arm, alarm in her voice. "What's wrong? Are you under a curse?"

"Not a curse... it's—argh..."

Another wave crashes over me. My soul rattles inside my skull, flipping between the dark stone corridor and the empty white cube hundreds of times a second.

"What is causing this?" Kate asks, her grip tightening. "Is it physical pain, or magical backlash?"

}
choice("Agree to Melindra's proposition.", B3-Ch10a-Proposition, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Proposition, v_b3_ch10_benefits = 1) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1)
choice("Decline Melindra's proposition.", B3-Ch10a-Proposition, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Proposition, v_b3_ch10_benefits = 0) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1)
choice("Tell Kate the truth about what's happening to me.", B3-Ch10a-Abyss, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Abyss) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1)
choice("Tell Kate that it's just a normal headache and that she shouldn't worry about it.", B3-Ch10a-Headache, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Headache) if (v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Proposition
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch10_benefits,1) if (v_b3_ch10_benefits == 1)
achievement("Friends with benefits",v_ac_b3_ch10_benefits)
#if(v_b3_ch10_benefits == 1) {
"Sounds reasonable to me," I say. "Let's give it a try."

"Splendid," Melindra smiles.

"Does that mean we share a tent from tonight?" I ask.

"Not while traveling with the others!" Melindra laughs. "That would be awkward."

"You didn't mind before," I note.

"That was before Kate and I were friends," Melindra says. "Circumstances change."

"Don't alter your plans on my account," Kate says quietly.

"We have plenty of time ahead," Melindra says, patting my arm. "No need to cause friction during the tournament. Right, Barry?"

"Right," I say.

"Next lever," Melindra says, walking down the corridor to pull the left handle.

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_benefits == 0) {
"I don't think that kind of arrangement suits me," I say.

"Suit yourself," Melindra shrugs without missing a beat.

"No hard feelings?"

"None at all," she says, turning toward the next lever.

}
A spike of agonizing pressure pierces my skull.

Vision violently shatters. My consciousness ricochets between the earthen and ethereal planes, flashing back and forth in rapid succession like a strobe light. Just as the rhythm slows, another surge hits—tossing my perspective between flesh and void more than ten times in a single heartbeat.

"Barry?" Kate grabs my arm, alarm in her voice. "What's wrong? Are you under a curse?"

"Not a curse... it's—argh..."

Another wave crashes over me. My soul rattles inside my skull, flipping between the dark stone corridor and the empty white cube hundreds of times a second.

"What is causing this?" Kate asks, her grip tightening. "Is it physical pain, or magical backlash?"

choice("Tell Kate the truth about what's happening to me.", B3-Ch10a-Abyss, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Abyss)
choice("Tell Kate that it's just a normal headache and that she shouldn't worry about it.", B3-Ch10a-Headache, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Headache)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Headache
TEXT:

"Just an ordinary headache," I grit out. "Nothing to worry about."

"Do you need to lie down?" Kate asks. "Will resting help?"

"I don't know, I—argh—"

"What's happening?" Melindra calls from down the hall.

"He's collapsing," Kate shouts.

"Is that what happened over the roofs?" Melindra asks. "He lost the winds to a blackout?"

"Barry, talk to me," Kate pleads, her voice trembling. "Barry!"

Her voice stretches, thin and distant. Darkness bleeds across my vision on both planes at once. Sensation drains from my limbs, my jaw locks, and the shadows pull me down into bottomless silence.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Abyss
TEXT:

"I don't know what's happening," I gasp, holding my temples. "My consciousness keeps tearing between the physical world and the ether. The friction is ripping my head apart. I can't anchor myself."

"No..." Flower gasps, horror in her voice. "Oh no. Barry, close your eyes! Shut them right now and keep them closed!"

"They're closed!" I squeeze my eyelids shut, darkness pressing in. "Now what?"

"I don't know!" Flower wails.

"What do you mean you don't know?"

"The abyss is trying to reclaim you," Flower says, panic edging her words.

"The ethereal abyss? The void you threw me into to escape the ether's attack?"

"Yes! You couldn't wake from there on your own. Petal had to douse your physical body with cold water to break the trance. We thought that severed the link, but it wasn't enough!"

"What does that mean?" I ask, keeping my eyes clamped shut. "What do I do?"

"Maybe... you could live the rest of your life with your eyes closed?" Flower offers timidly.

"What?" I shout.

"I'm sorry! This has never happened before! As long as your eyes stay closed on both planes, the void cannot pull you under."

She pauses.

"You have them closed in the ether too, right?"

"How should I know?" I snap. "I can't check without opening them!"

"Can't you feel it with your aura?" Flower asks.

"Ethereal and earthen auras feel identical to me! I don't know what to look for! Isn't there anything else?"

"Well... you're still alive, which is good..." Flower's voice wavers. "Maybe if we—"

"Calm down," Illuna's crisp tone cuts through the panic. "Think rationally. The void cannot influence the ether any more than the ether can manipulate the void. If the abyss were reaching for him directly, he would already be gone."

"Then what is happening?" Flower asks.

"A fragment of void essence must have hitched a ride on his soul when he returned," Illuna reasons. "It has attached itself to the exterior of his sanctuary and is attempting to breach the boundary. If you reach his sanctuary in the ether, your fire can burn it away."

"I don't know the coordinates of Barry's mind!" Flower says. "We never reached it during the lesson!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Emergency, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Emergency)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Emergency
TEXT:

"Barry, listen carefully," Illuna commands. "Your eyes are closed, but your hearing and touch remain intact. The floor of your sanctuary feels distinct from stone. Sit on the ground and press your palms flat. As your consciousness shifts between planes, the texture beneath your hands will change."

"Understood," I say, dropping to the flagstones and pressing my hands against the rock.

Immediately, the rough, chilly stone beneath my palms pulses, alternating between cold granite and a warm, smooth surface unlike anything on the material plane.

"I feel it," I say. "The shift is continuous. What next?"

"Channel magical energy through your palms," Illuna says. "Visualize the power radiating across the entire exterior of your cube in the ether."

"I can do that," I say, focusing intent into my hands.

"Flower, transform and scan the ether for a sanctuary leaking raw mana," Illuna instructs.

"You said I shouldn't transform again—"

"This is an emergency," Illuna cuts her off. "Shift now."

"Right! Doing it!"

"I hate to interrupt," Melindra calls from down the corridor, "but if I stop pulling levers, the defense grid will trigger and turn us into pincushions."

"Can you stall the timer?" Kate asks.

"I'm already stalling it," Melindra says. "The maximum delay between levers is five minutes. If you aren't finished in twenty minutes, we abandon the ruin."

"Can't we return later?" Kate asks.

"The mechanism locks out for a month once triggered," Melindra replies. "Move quickly."

"I'm on it," Flower says. "I sense the signature! Teleporting now!"

Five seconds later, Flower's triumphant voice rings out.

"Found it! I'm at Barry's cube!"

"Good," Illuna says. "Do you see dark residue along the edges or corners?"

"Yes! A black stain creeping across the seams! I'm burning it now!"

"Cleanse every inch," Illuna says. "Notify us when clear so Barry can open his eyes."

"Almost... almost... Done! It's gone!"

choice("Open my eyes.", B3-Ch10a-Open, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Open)
choice("Ask Flower to check the whole surface of the cube, in case there are more shadows.", B3-Ch10a-Shadows, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Shadows)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Open
TEXT:

"Finally," I breathe, opening my eyes. "Now we can—wait. Where is everyone?"

The corridor is gone. Cold void stretches in every direction. Lethargy washes over my limbs like lead, dragging my thoughts down. Darkness swallows my senses, and consciousness slips away for good.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Shadows
TEXT:

"Can you scan the rest of the cube's surface?" I call out. "If one shadow took root, others could be hiding."

"I didn't spot anything else, but I'll make a thorough sweep," Flower says. "Give me a moment!"

Silence hangs for twenty seconds while she inspects the perimeter.

"Wait... I think there's another smudge near the base..." Flower says. "Burning it now!"

"Can you engulf the entire cube in fire?" I suggest. "That leaves nothing to chance."

"Is that safe?" Flower asks. "Will it damage your mind?"

"It should hold," Illuna says. "Sanctuary walls are engineered to withstand immense magical pressure. Blanket the surface."

"Understood!"

Muffled roars echo from the ether—Flower unleashing waves of ethereal flame against the exterior of my cube. After several seconds, the noise subsides.

"All clear," Flower announces. "Barry, open your eyes."

"Finally," I say, blinking against the torchlight. "Now we can focus on the—wait. What is this?"

"What's wrong?" Flower asks.

"My perspective is shifting, but... under control," I murmur. "Every time I blink with intent, I slide between the material corridor and the ethereal plane. I can move my consciousness at will. Should I be concerned?"

"Is the shift involuntary, or do you command it?" Illuna asks.

"I command it," I say, testing a series of deliberate blinks. "I can cross over, or blink normally without triggering the transition. I toggle it through concentration. How did this happen?"

"How many times did your consciousness oscillate between planes during the seizure?" Illuna asks.

"Dozens of times a second before I shut my eyes," I calculate. "Thousands of transitions. Maybe tens of thousands."

"Then the boundary reflex burned itself into your instincts," Illuna concludes. "Enjoy your newfound capability."

"Does this make me an ethereal?" I ask, brow furrowing.

"Dual-plane awareness is not exclusive to ethereals," Illuna says. "Arraka and I do the same, as does any high spirit. The distinction lies in primary orientation. Flower considers her true self to reside in her ethereal sanctuary, viewing her earthen body as a puppet on strings. Do you perceive your flesh as a vessel?"

"No," I say, flexing my fingers. "My physical body remains my primary anchor. The ethereal form feels like the projection."

"Then you are not an ethereal," Illuna says simply.

"Fair enough," I say.

"Fascinating as this is," Melindra calls out, "the defense timer is ticking. Either collect your puzzle piece or let's leave this tomb before the darts fly!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Lever, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Lever)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Lever
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_b3_ch10_puzzle == 0 && v_b3_ch10_spin == 1 || v_b3_ch10_puzzle == 1 && v_b3_ch10_spin == 1 && v_perception >= 2 || v_b3_ch10_puzzle == 1 && v_b3_ch10_spin == 1 && v_reflexes >= 3 || v_b3_ch10_spin == 0)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_b3_ch10_puzzle == 1 && v_b3_ch10_spin == 1 && v_perception < 2 && v_reflexes < 3)
#if(v_b3_ch10_spin == 0) {
"Alright, sorry!" Flower calls out. "When is the final lever?"

"Right after this one," Melindra says. "Are you in position?"

"Yes! Where do I stand again?"

"Next to the—"

Every torch in the corridor snuffs out simultaneously, plunging the hall into pitch darkness.

"Damn these ancient sconces!" Melindra curses. "I knew the pitch wouldn't hold!"

"Don't worry," Flower says. "I'll raise fire orbs."

"As I was saying," Melindra continues as three golden spheres ignite and rise to the ceiling, casting long shadows, "to receive the fragment, stand against that wall while I pull the final handle. The partition will slide back, revealing the dispenser button—gods above, why is Arraka shrieking like a madwoman?"

"Ignore her," Illuna says as frantic buzzing rattles the locket. "She is cursing us again."

"She hasn't made a sound for half an hour," Kate points out. "What if it's an actual warning? Open the latch, Illuna."

"Fine," Illuna says, thumbing the catch. "Brace your ears."

"You blind, bumbling fools!" Arraka's screech bounces off the stones. "The illusionist has been stalking you under an invisibility cloak! He killed the torches and warped your spatial orientation with a mirror hex while the lights were out! Look at the walls! You're facing the opposite direction!"

"How would he have tracked us here?" Illuna demands.

"She's right," Melindra gasps, scanning the masonry. "The corridor is nearly symmetrical, but the stone grain—"

"Get away from the walls!" Arraka shrieks. "He's throwing a lever!"

"Move!" Melindra shouts. "To me! This flagstone is the only safe pocket!"

We lunge toward Melindra as stone panels snap open along the corridor. Hundreds of iron-tipped quarrels whistle through the air, while circular blades saw their way up through the floor, grinding along grooved tracks. Kate drops four thick ice barriers around our position, deflecting the storm of projectiles with sharp clatters.

"Where is he?" Kate asks, breathing hard. "Is he still in the chamber?"

"He's fleeing up the stairs," Arraka says.

"Up the stairs?" Melindra lets out a dark chuckle. "Well, that's the end of him."

"What do you mean?" I ask.

A strangled shriek cuts through the corridor as the stairwell erupts in a roaring geyser of flame.

"The exit has a retaliatory firewall," Melindra says. "He triggered the primary failsafe."

"Is he dead?" Flower asks softly.

"Reduced to cinders," Melindra says flatly.

"How long do we hold behind the ice?" Kate asks. "When do the mechanisms wind down?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_puzzle == 0 && v_b3_ch10_spin == 1) {
"Alright, sorry!" Flower calls out. "When is the final lever?"

"Right after this one," Melindra says. "Are you in position?"

"Yes! Where do I stand again?"

"Next to the—"

Every torch in the corridor snuffs out simultaneously, plunging the hall into pitch darkness.

"Damn these ancient sconces!" Melindra curses. "I knew the pitch wouldn't hold!"

"Don't worry," Flower says. "I'll raise fire orbs."

"As I was saying," Melindra continues as three golden spheres ignite and rise to the ceiling, casting long shadows, "to receive the fragment, stand against that wall while I pull the final handle. The partition will slide back, revealing the dispenser button. Got it?"

"Got it!" Flower trots over to the designated alcove.

"Pulling on three!" Melindra shouts from the far end. "One, two, three!"

The lever clunks down—and iron quarrels erupt from the stonework. Flower flips backward, dodging three shafts in a blur of acrobatics. Kate raises floating ice shields against the volley, but grinding gears shriek beneath our feet as spinning saw blades rise through the floorboards.

"Fall back to me!" Melindra screams. "The flagstone beneath my feet is clear!"

We sprint through the hail of bolts. Kate throws up four interlocking ice walls around Melindra's position, sealing us behind frozen barricades as arrows splinter against the frost.

"What happened?" Flower gasps.

"The corridor was inverted!" Melindra seethes. "An illusion flipped our perspective when the torches died! Barry, didn't you mention a royal guard with optical magic?"

"The invisible illusionist," I say. "He was untraceable."

"If I catch him, I'll tear his—"

A horrific scream echoes from the stairwell as a column of roaring fire engulfs the exit above.

"Never mind," Melindra exhales. "The stair traps caught him. Problem solved."

"Is he dead?" Flower asks softly.

"Reduced to cinders," Melindra says flatly.

"How long do we hold behind the ice?" Kate asks. "When do the mechanisms wind down?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_puzzle == 1 && v_b3_ch10_spin == 1 && v_perception >= 2) {
"Alright, sorry!" Flower calls out. "When is the final lever?"

"Right after this one," Melindra says. "Are you in position?"

"Yes! Where do I stand again?"

"Next to the—"

Every torch in the corridor snuffs out simultaneously, plunging the hall into pitch darkness.

"Damn these ancient sconces!" Melindra curses. "I knew the pitch wouldn't hold!"

"Don't worry," Flower says. "I'll raise fire orbs."

"As I was saying," Melindra continues as three golden spheres ignite and rise to the ceiling, casting long shadows, "to receive the fragment, stand against that wall while I pull the final handle. The partition will slide back, revealing the dispenser button. Got it?"

"Got it!" Flower trots over to the wall. "Barry, step up if you want one too."

"Coming," I say, walking toward her position.

"Pulling on three!" Melindra shouts from the far end. "One, two, th—"

"Hold!" I yell. "Don't pull it!"

"Why?" Melindra stops.

"Look at the masonry," I say, scanning the walls. "That scored stone was on the opposite side earlier. The short lever was on the left wall, not the right. And the hairline fracture in that mortar was beside the stairs. The room has been mirrored."

"You're right," Melindra says, narrowing her eyes. "The layout is almost symmetrical, but the details are reversed... Barry, didn't you say the surviving guard was an illusionist?"

"The invisible one," I say.

"Invisible?" Melindra's eyes dart down the hall just as an iron lever drops with a heavy clack by unseen hands.

"Move!" Melindra shouts. "To me! Quick!"

We lunge toward Melindra as stone panels snap open along the corridor. Hundreds of iron-tipped quarrels whistle through the air, while circular blades saw their way up through the floor, grinding along grooved tracks. Kate drops four thick ice barriers around our position, deflecting the storm of projectiles with sharp clatters.

"What happened?" Flower asks.

"The illusionist inverted the room," Melindra snarls. "He extinguished the sconces, cast a disorientation field, and pulled a trap trigger when we caught on. He's probably gloating—"

A horrific scream echoes from the stairwell as a column of roaring fire engulfs the exit above.

"Never mind," Melindra exhales. "He ran into the stairwell firewall. He's not laughing anymore."

"Is he dead?" Flower asks softly.

"Reduced to cinders," Melindra says flatly.

"How long do we hold behind the ice?" Kate asks. "When do the mechanisms wind down?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_puzzle == 1 && v_b3_ch10_spin == 1 && v_perception < 2) {
"Alright, sorry!" Flower calls out. "When is the final lever?"

"Right after this one," Melindra says. "Are you in position?"

"Yes! Where do I stand again?"

"Next to the—"

Every torch in the corridor snuffs out simultaneously, plunging the hall into pitch darkness.

"Damn these ancient sconces!" Melindra curses. "I knew the pitch wouldn't hold!"

"Don't worry," Flower says. "I'll raise fire orbs."

"As I was saying," Melindra continues as three golden spheres ignite and rise to the ceiling, casting long shadows, "to receive the fragment, stand against that wall while I pull the final handle. The partition will slide back, revealing the dispenser button. Got it?"

"Got it!" Flower trots over to the wall. "Barry, step up if you want one too."

"Coming," I say, heading toward her.

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_puzzle == 1 && v_b3_ch10_spin == 1 && v_reflexes >= 3 && v_perception < 2) {
"Pulling on three!" Melindra shouts from the far end. "One, two, three!"

The lever clunks down—and a volley of iron quarrels shrieks straight at my chest.

I throw myself sideways by raw reflex, rolling across the flagstones as steel tips spark against the stone where I stood. Circular saw blades rip up through the floor, gnashing along metal tracks. Flower somersaults over spinning teeth while Kate raises defensive ice barriers.

"Fall back to me!" Melindra screams. "The flagstone beneath my feet is clear!"

We sprint through the hail of bolts. Kate throws up four interlocking ice walls around Melindra's position, sealing us behind frozen barricades as arrows splinter against the frost.

"What happened?" Flower gasps.

"The corridor was inverted!" Melindra seethes. "An illusion flipped our perspective when the torches died! Barry, didn't you mention a royal guard with optical magic?"

"The invisible illusionist," I say. "He was untraceable."

"If I catch him, I'll tear his—"

A horrific scream echoes from the stairwell as a column of roaring fire engulfs the exit above.

"Never mind," Melindra exhales. "The stair traps caught him. Problem solved."

"Is he dead?" Flower asks softly.

"Reduced to cinders," Melindra says flatly.

"How long do we hold behind the ice?" Kate asks. "When do the mechanisms wind down?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_puzzle == 1 && v_b3_ch10_spin == 1 && v_perception < 2 && v_reflexes < 3) {
"Pulling on three!" Melindra shouts from the far end. "One, two, three!"

The lever clunks down—and iron quarrels erupt from the wall directly in front of me. I have no time to duck. An arrow punches through my eye socket, and everything goes black.

}
