choice("Ask Flower if she can also check the inside of my sanctuary for shades before she leaves.", B3-Ch10a-Check, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Check, v_b3_ch10_check = 1)
choice("Let Flower leave.", B3-Ch10a-Check, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Check, v_b3_ch10_check = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Check
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_check == 1) {
"Flower, can you inspect the inside of my sanctuary before leaving?" I ask.

"You are already inside," Illuna points out. "Inspect it yourself."

"She recognizes the void residue," I say. "What if I mistake an ordinary shadow for an abyss fragment?"

"I'll take a quick look," Flower agrees.

"Flower, that is unnecessary—" Illuna starts, but Flower cuts in.

"Barry, I'm here!" Flower calls from the ether.

"Hold on, switching over."

I focus intent, blink, and my perspective drops into the ethereal cube.

"Your room is awfully plain," Flower observes, glancing around the barren white walls. "No table? Not even a bench?"

"I'll conjure furniture once I master the spell," I say.

"I'll teach you later!" Flower promises. "No shadows in here, Barry. You're completely safe. Heading back to my mind now. See you soon!"

She vanishes in a ripple of flame.

I blink, returning my awareness to my physical eyes in the dusty townhouse, and dial back the shifting reflex so ordinary blinks don't pull me across planes.

"Ugh... I feel a bit dizzy," Flower mumbles, her eyes fading back to soft green.

"I warned you," Illuna's voice chides from within. "Teleporting into foreign sanctuaries exhausts the vessel. Consider yourself fortunate a mild headache is your only penalty."

"Sorry," I offer. "I didn't realize."

"I'm going to rest," Flower says, her eyelids drooping. "Petal, take the helm."

"Understood," Illuna says, and the irises shift to calm blue.

"Ready to depart?" Melindra asks.

"I will need assistance aloft," Illuna says. "I cannot generate fire jets."

"My ice platform will support you," Kate offers. "You won't add much weight."

"Thank you," Illuna says.

"We meet Daren, Hadrik, and Leila in the market district," Melindra says. "Follow me."

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_check == 0) {
"I'm back," Flower announces a moment later, stepping out of her trance. "Reverting now!"

She plants her feet and concentrates, the crimson sparks fading until her irises return to bright green.

"Done!"

"Are you alright?" Illuna checks. "No strain from the shift?"

"I feel fine," Flower smiles.

"Good," Illuna says. "Shall we rejoin the others?"

"Time to meet Daren, Hadrik, and Leila at the market," Melindra says. "Follow my lead."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Caverns, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Caverns) if (v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Safely, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Safely) if (v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Caverns
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_kelrim != 1 && v_b3_ch6_plan != 3) {
After twenty minutes aloft, we touch down beside our companions, who have finished gathering provisions. Once we recount the skirmish with the illusionist in the ruins, we purchase rations and medical supplies, then signal the rebel technician to teleport us back to the cavern base.

For the next two hours, we make the rounds through the underground network, offering farewells to the allies we fought beside. We bid goodbye to Breynan, Peter, Albert, Desmond, Reidzek, and even Barry Thundercloud in his holding cell.

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 1 && v_b3_ch6_kelrim == 1 || v_b3_ch2_goblins == 1 && v_b3_ch6_plan == 3) {
After twenty minutes aloft, we touch down beside our companions, who have finished gathering provisions. Once we recount the skirmish with the illusionist in the ruins, we purchase rations and medical supplies, then signal the rebel technician to teleport us back to the cavern base.

For the next two hours, we make the rounds through the underground network, offering farewells to the allies we fought beside. We bid goodbye to Breynan, Peter, Albert, Desmond, Reidzek, Kelrim, and even Barry Thundercloud in his holding cell.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_kelrim == 1 && v_b3_ch2_goblins != 1 || v_b3_ch6_plan == 3 && v_b3_ch2_goblins != 1) {
After twenty minutes aloft, we touch down beside our companions, who have finished gathering provisions. Once we recount the skirmish with the illusionist in the ruins, we purchase rations and medical supplies, then signal the rebel technician to teleport us back to the cavern base.

For the next two hours, we make the rounds through the underground network, offering farewells to the allies we fought beside. We bid goodbye to Breynan, Peter, Albert, Desmond, Reidzek, Kelrim, Kelrim's goblin crew, and even Barry Thundercloud in his holding cell.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_cut == 0) {
As we approach our quarters, we spot young Alice—the girl who pleaded for her family on our first night in the tunnels—embracing a woman who must be her mother. Albert stands nearby, explaining that her brother and father have also been recovered from the seredium facility and are resting under healer care in the infirmary.

"Look," Hadrik murmurs, watching from a distance. "Her family made it out."

"I'm glad," I say. "Her father was in rough shape when we saw him in the tower cells."

"The white mages here are skilled," Daren says. "They'll restore him."

When we reach our sleeping quarters, we sit in a circle to plan our final hours before morning.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_cut == 1) {
As we approach our quarters, we spot young Alice—the girl who pleaded for her family on our first night in the tunnels—embracing her weeping mother. Albert hesitates before delivering the news: while her brother survived, her father died in the seredium facility before the rescue teams arrived.

Memory hits me like a fist—the man's body convulsing as the severed chains released their fatal magical backlash. Nausea rises in my throat.

"Inside, Barry," Daren says softly, placing a steady hand on my shoulder. "You don't need to listen to this."

As we head to our quarters, the child's wailing echoes down the stone corridor, fading only when we are too far down the hall to hear her voice.

When we reach our sleeping quarters, we sit in a circle to plan our final hours before morning.

}
With tomorrow's dawn marking our departure from Ollendor, dividing our remaining time with Leila takes negotiation. We reach a consensus: Daren, Hadrik, Melindra, Flower, and Illuna will visit with her through the evening, while Kate and I take the night watches so we can bid proper farewells.

Leila waves off our concerns about her missing sleep; she has nowhere to march tomorrow and can rest once we depart.

While the others gather around her to reminisce, I sit in the corner and practice my dual-plane shifting, determined to master the transition before we take to the open road.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Flow, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Flow)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Safely
TEXT:

After twenty minutes aloft, we touch down beside our companions, who have finished gathering provisions. Once we recount the skirmish with the illusionist in the ruins, we purchase extra rations and seek out lodging. Returning to the rebel base is impossible; showing up unscathed after the Overseer's victory would draw dangerous suspicion.

Finding an operating tavern in a city recovering from martial lockdown takes over an hour, but we eventually secure a large private chamber with multiple cots.

With tomorrow's dawn marking our departure from Ollendor, dividing our remaining time with Leila takes negotiation. We reach a consensus: Daren, Hadrik, Melindra, Flower, and Illuna will visit with her through the evening, while Kate and I take the night watches so we can bid proper farewells.

Leila waves off our concerns about her missing sleep; she has nowhere to march tomorrow and can rest once we depart.

While the others gather around her to reminisce, I sit in the corner and practice my dual-plane shifting, determined to master the transition before we take to the open road.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Flow, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Flow)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Flow
TEXT:

It takes over an hour of disciplined focus, but I eventually establish simultaneous awareness across both planes. The ethereal perspective hovers in the back of my mind like peripheral vision—a secondary layer I can monitor without abandoning physical reality.

My physical vessel is no longer vulnerable to sneak attacks while my soul explores the ether, and I can hear companion voices clearly in the room. I also discard the blinking trigger, anchoring the shift entirely to conscious intent.

With the framework in place, I ask Flower if she feels rested enough to manifest outside my sanctuary for spell drills. Illuna promptly vetoes the request; another transformation today risks burning the vessel, so training must wait until tomorrow.

#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
When the evening finally draws to a close, the camp falls quiet. Kate and Leila take the first watch in the tunnel outside our quarters. Hours later, a gentle shake from Kate wakes me—Leila is waiting down the hall.

After changing out of my sleeping clothes and grabbing my pack, I walk out into the corridor. Leila sits with her back against the rough cavern wall, far enough from the barracks to speak without waking the sleepers while keeping sight of the tunnel junction.

I sit beside her, but from the opening words, the rhythm falters. An unfamiliar stiffness hangs between us. We both know this conversation marks the end of our travels together, and the finality presses down like lead.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
When the evening finally draws to a close, the inn falls quiet. Kate and Leila take the first watch in the street below. Hours later, a gentle shake from Kate wakes me—Leila is waiting across the square.

After changing out of my sleeping clothes and grabbing my pack, I head downstairs into the cool night air. Leila sits against the shuttered front of a merchant shop directly across from our lodging.

I sit beside her, but from the opening words, the rhythm falters. An unfamiliar stiffness hangs between us. We both know this conversation marks the end of our travels together, and the finality presses down like lead.

}
When will our paths cross again? What if the tournament turns lethal and I never return? What if I say something careless and leave it as her lasting memory of me?

The thoughts clutter my head, choking off easy conversation. After a few halting exchanges, we lapse into complete silence—an awkward wall that has never stood between us, despite her muteness.

I have to break the deadlock.

choice("Offer Leila a biscuit.", B3-Ch10a-Divided, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Divided, v_b3_ch10_biscuit = 1)
choice("Ask Leila if she knows the joke about the rock golem and the three-legged trampler.", B3-Ch10a-Divided, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Divided, v_b3_ch10_biscuit = 0)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Divided
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_biscuit == 1) {
I reach into my pack, pull out a dry biscuit, and hold it out.

"Here," I say. "Have a biscuit."

"Thank you," Leila writes in glowing blue letters, taking the ration and nibbling the corner.

"Savor it," I say. "Supplies are dwindling."

"I know," she writes with a faint smile, taking small bites until the crumb is gone.

When she finishes, the silence rushes right back in. We sit side by side on the cold ground, neither of us finding a thread.

Ten seconds stretch into twenty. Unable to bear the vacuum, I blurt out the first thought in my head.

"Has your stat booster accumulated points lately? I haven't received a single distribution since we entered Ollendor."

"No," Leila writes. "Nothing since we arrived."

"Something in the cities must dampen the intake," I muse. "We noticed the same lag in Thilias."

"We did," she writes.

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_biscuit == 0) {
"Do you know the joke about the rock golem and the three-legged trampler?" I ask.

"Everyone knows that joke, Barry," Leila writes in glowing blue script.

"Right," I mutter.

The attempt falls flat. Silence rushes right back in, heavy and awkward.

Ten seconds stretch into twenty. Unable to bear the vacuum, I blurt out the first thought in my head.

"Has your stat booster accumulated points lately? I haven't received a single distribution since we entered Ollendor."

"No," Leila writes. "Nothing since we arrived."

"Something in the cities must dampen the intake," I muse. "We noticed the same lag in Thilias."

"We did," she writes.

}
"What if the drain is concentration?" I hypothesize. "If dozens of stat devices are operating within city limits, the ambient mana draw splits between them."

"Unlikely," Leila writes. "My booster tracks proximity signatures for other devices. I would have detected them in the streets."

"Unless they're stationary," I argue. "A stockpile of devices sequestered in a vault to siphon power from participants would emit a localized signature you wouldn't cross on regular patrols."

"Perhaps," she writes softly.

"And another detail nags at me," I continue, leaning in. "Melindra searched the western continent for stat devices and found merchant shelves stripped clean, even though they used to be common fortune-teller novelties. What if an organization bought every unit prior to the tournament to—"

"Barry..." Leila's glowing script cuts across my vision.

I look up. Her hazel eyes are fixed directly on mine.

"What are we doing?"

"I'm... not sure what you mean—"

"This may be our last night together," her letters float between us, sharp and bright, "and we are analyzing magical hardware."

"What would you rather talk about?" I ask.

"Anything," Leila writes. "Anything that doesn't feel like a forced technical lecture. Barry, what happened to us? We used to talk about whatever crossed our minds without this wall between us. Why can't we treat tonight like any other watch? We aren't parting forever. We are taking a short recess."

"You're right," I say, the tension in my chest loosening. "You're completely right. I don't know why I was overcomplicating it. We're parting for a few weeks, maybe a month, and then we return. Friends don't stage funeral rites over a brief trip."

"Exactly," Leila writes. "So let's talk like ourselves."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Appropriate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Appropriate) if (v_b3_ch8_heartfelt == 2)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Letters, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Letters) if (v_b3_ch8_heartfelt != 2)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Appropriate
TEXT:

"You've convinced me," I smile. "What's our topic?"

"Doesn't matter," Leila writes, her eyes crinkling. "I intend to enjoy every second of it."

She reaches over, taking my hand in hers. Her grip is warm and steady. We talk about our first chaotic days in Thilias, the arena cells, and every absurd moment on the road. The awkwardness evaporates, replaced by the easy familiarity we built across hundreds of miles.

The hours slip away unnoticed, but neither of us wastes a breath. Whatever challenges lie ahead in the tournament, this memory will remain untarnished.

"Have you settled on your plans after we depart?" I ask. "Will you remain underground, or find a home on the surface?"

#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"I'll stay with the resistance for a brief period," Leila writes, "but the administration is moving to the upper city. Father and I plan to lease a townhouse in the residential quarter and make a fresh start."

"Living under the same roof again," I smile.

"That is the plan," she writes.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
"I cannot return to the caverns," Leila writes. "It's too dangerous. I spoke with Father yesterday about our pact with the Overseer, and we agreed to relocate to the surface. We have a rendezvous point arranged for this evening."

"Will the rebels let him leave?" I ask.

"They will," Leila writes with quiet certainty. "They owe him too much to detain him, and as far as they know, we were separated during the Overseer's assault. His departure to search for me will arouse no suspicion."

"True," I say.

}
A gentle pause settles between us—not awkward this time, but peaceful. We both recognize the night watch is drawing to its close.

"Barry... before the sun rises... there is one thing," Leila writes, a faint flush coloring her cheeks. "The other night, when you promised we would travel together and I... kissed you... I know it was sudden, but I was so happy that I acted without thinking. I hope I didn't make things difficult between us..."

choice("Tell Leila that there's no need to apologize because she didn't do anything inappropriate.", B3-Ch10a-Apologize, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Apologize, v_b3_ch10_inappropriate = 0)
choice("Tell Leila that even though it was a little out of the blue, it wasn't a problem.", B3-Ch10a-Apologize, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Apologize, v_b3_ch10_inappropriate = 1)
choice("Lean in and kiss Leila, then tell her that now we are even.", B3-Ch10a-Apologize, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Apologize, v_b3_ch10_inappropriate = 2)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Apologize
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_inappropriate == 0) {
"Leila, it's fine," I say. "You didn't do anything inappropriate. There's no need for you to apologize."

"I'm glad you feel that way," Leila writes with a shy smile.

She pauses, the color deepening on her cheeks.

"Would it be too sudden if I kissed you again now?"

"Not at all," I say.

"Good," she writes, her eyes shining. "Consider yourself warned."

She leans in and presses her lips against mine, soft and lingering.

"I'll miss you, Barry..." she writes as she pulls back.

"I'll miss you too," I murmur.

"Dawn is breaking," Leila writes, rising to her feet. "We should wake the others."

With those final words, we return to the room together in comfortable silence.

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_inappropriate == 1) {
"I'll admit that it was a little out of the blue, but it wasn't a problem," I say.

"I'm glad it didn't cause discomfort," Leila writes with a small smile.

"Not in the slightest," I say. "Don't worry about it."

"We should wake the others," Leila writes, standing up and dusting off her leggings.

After a few paces, she turns back, blue letters forming in the dim air.

"I'll miss you, Barry..."

"I'll miss you too," I say.

With those final words, we return to the room together in comfortable silence.

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_inappropriate == 2) {
Instead of answering, I lean across the space between us and press a quick kiss to her lips.

"Don't worry about it," I say as she looks at me in surprise. "Now we're even."

"Now we're even!" Leila writes with a bright smile.

After a quiet moment, she rises to her feet, blue script drifting before me.

"Dawn is breaking. Time to wake the others."

"Right," I say, standing up. "Let's go."

She takes a few steps, then glances over her shoulder.

"I'll miss you, Barry..."

"I'll miss you too," I say.

With those final words, we return to the room together in comfortable silence.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch10a-Section-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch10a-Section-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch10a-Letters
TEXT:

"You've convinced me," I smile. "What's our topic?"

"Doesn't matter," Leila writes, her eyes crinkling. "I intend to enjoy every second of it."

We relax against the wall, reminiscing about our first days in Thilias, the arena, and the road. The awkwardness evaporates, replaced by the easy familiarity we built across hundreds of miles.

The hours slip away unnoticed, but neither of us wastes a breath. Whatever challenges lie ahead in the tournament, this memory will remain untarnished.

"Have you settled on your plans after we depart?" I ask. "Will you remain underground, or find a home on the surface?"

#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"I'll stay with the resistance for a brief period," Leila writes, "but the administration is moving to the upper city. Father and I plan to lease a townhouse in the residential quarter and make a fresh start."

"Living under the same roof again," I smile.

"That is the plan," she writes.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
"I cannot return to the caverns," Leila writes. "It's too dangerous. I spoke with Father yesterday about our pact with the Overseer, and we agreed to relocate to the surface. We have a rendezvous point arranged for this evening."

"Will the rebels let him leave?" I ask.

"They will," Leila writes with quiet certainty. "They owe him too much to detain him, and as far as they know, we were separated during the Overseer's assault. His departure to search for me will arouse no suspicion."

"True," I say.

}
A gentle pause settles between us. We both recognize the night watch is drawing to its close, and we accept it without regret.

"Dawn is breaking," Leila writes, rising to her feet. "Time to wake the others."

